<dc:title>McMillan Plan</dc:title>
<dc:description>The McMillan Plan was &lt;a href=&quot;http://mallhistory.org/items/show/206&quot;&gt;presented to Congress and the public&lt;/a&gt; by its authors, the &lt;a href=&quot;http://mallhistory.org/items/show/179&quot;&gt;Senate Park Commission&lt;/a&gt;, in 1902. It described a comprehensive plan redesigning not only the National Mall but the entire system of parks in Washington, DC. Their proposed redesign of the Mall included removing the &lt;a href=&quot;http://mallhistory.org/items/show/42&quot;&gt;train station&lt;/a&gt; from near 6th street and unifying the landscape between the &lt;a href=&quot;http://mallhistory.org/items/show/59&quot;&gt;Capitol&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href=&quot;http://mallhistory.org/items/show/36&quot;&gt;Washington Monument&lt;/a&gt;.</dc:description>
